# Options for /etc/init.d/dropbear. Sourced by the init script.
#
# DROPBEAR_EXTRA_ARGS is appended to the daemon's command line. It is the one
# place dropbear options belong: the init script has always sourced this file,
# and an earlier attempt to add a separate DROPBEAR_DISABLE_PASSWORD_AUTH
# variable was removed for being a second mechanism for a flag the script could
# already pass (see the commit "drop the invented password-auth switch").
#
# EMPTY MEANS PASSWORD LOGINS ARE ALLOWED, and that is deliberate here. A
# freshly flashed or factory-reset device has no authorized_keys, so shipping
# `-s` would install a package and leave SSH unreachable with no way in. The
# security that does NOT depend on this is in the init script itself: the
# port-22 accept is bound to the LAN interfaces (bridge0, ecm0, wlan0, wlan1)
# and new connections are DROPped everywhere else, so the carrier cannot reach
# it either way.
#
# For key-only authentication once you have a key installed:
#     DROPBEAR_EXTRA_ARGS="-s"
#
# Note what -s also does, because it is not obvious: it sets svr_opts.noauthpass,
# which is one of the guards on 600-allow-blank-root-password. With passwords
# allowed, root's password field is the only thing standing between a device
# whose install-time password step failed and a passwordless root login. See the
# note at the top of this package's Makefile.
DROPBEAR_EXTRA_ARGS=""

# Port, or [address:]port. /etc/init.d/dropbear derives the firewall rules from
# this too, stripping any address prefix. The web UI writes it here rather than
# editing the init script, which it used to do with a `sed` that corrupted the
# script - see ee71-webui's ssh.cgi.
#DROPBEAR_PORT=22
